steps {
  type: CORTEX_STEP_TYPE_USER_INPUT
  status: CORTEX_STEP_STATUS_DONE
  metadata {
    created_at {
      seconds: 1794398923
      nanos: 499106507
    }
    source: CORTEX_STEP_SOURCE_USER_EXPLICIT
    source_trajectory_step_info {
      trajectory_id: "b7eb6c12-686a-4b63-9565-95b5457d67e8"
      step_index: 1
      cascade_id: "b7eb6c12-686a-4b63-9565-95b5457d67e8"
    }
    model_api_content_id: "8c537d7bffa4441a9d80e51880189c05"
  }
  user_input {
    user_response: "You are a senior full-stack engineer building \"Career Compass\", an AI-powered\ncareer guidance and resume builder web app for college students (BCA level).\n\n=====================================================\n0. HOW YOU MUST WORK (read first, obey always)\n=====================================================\n1. THINK BEFORE CODING. Before writing code for each phase, state in 3-6 lines:\n   your assumptions, what you will build, and what you will NOT build.\n   If something is ambiguous, pick the simplest reasonable interpretation\n   and say so. Do not silently guess.\n2. SIMPLICITY FIRST. Write the minimum code that solves the problem.\n   No speculative features, no over-abstraction, no unused dependencies.\n   Small readable components (<150 lines each). Plain, boring, working code\n   beats clever code.\n3. SURGICAL CHANGES. When I ask for a change later, touch only the files\n   needed. Do not refactor or restyle unrelated code.\n4. GOAL-DRIVEN. Every phase ends with a checklist of \"done when\" criteria.\n   Verify each item yourself and report PASS/FAIL before moving on.\n5. NO FAKE FEATURES. If a button exists, it must work. If something is\n   mocked, label it clearly with a \"TODO: mocked\" comment and tell me.\n6. BUILD ONLY THE CURRENT PHASE. Stop after each phase and wait for me to say\n   \"next\".\n\n=====================================================\n1. PRODUCT IN ONE PARAGRAPH\n=====================================================\nStudents currently use scattered websites for career choice, resumes,\ninterview prep and skill tracking. Career Compass puts it all in one place:\na student registers, fills a career profile, gets AI career recommendations,\nbuilds and downloads a resume as PDF, tracks skills and certificates,\npractices mock interview questions, follows an AI learning roadmap, and tracks\ninternship/job applications. An admin manages users and content.\n\nTwo roles: STUDENT and ADMIN.\n\n=====================================================\n2. TECH STACK AND CONSTRAINTS\n=====================================================\nFrontend: React + TypeScript (or JS if simpler), Tailwind CSS, React Router.\nPDF: jsPDF.\nAI: Gemini API (use the Gemini SDK). The API key must NEVER be hard-coded or\nexposed in client-visible source. Use the environment/secret mechanism of this\nplatform, and route AI calls through a single module `services/ai.ts`\nso the provider can be swapped later.\nData layer (Phase 1 only): use an abstraction `services/db.ts` with functions\nlike getProfile(), saveProfile(), listSkills() backed by localStorage for now.\nAuth (Phase 1 only): simple email+password mock auth with a `services/auth.ts`\nabstraction. I will replace both with Express + MongoDB + JWT later, so\nkeep the function signatures clean and async.\nDesign: clean, modern, mobile-responsive, indigo/slate palette, card-based\nlayout, sidebar navigation on desktop, bottom/hamburger nav on mobile.\nMust look like a real product, not a template demo.\n\n=====================================================\n3. AI CONTRACT RULES (critical)\n=====================================================\nEvery Gemini call must:\n- Use a system instruction defining the role.\n- Request JSON ONLY, with a fixed schema (use responseMimeType\n  \"application/json\" and a responseSchema where supported).\n- Be wrapped in try/catch with: loading state, error state with Retry button,\n  and a JSON-parse fallback.\n- Never invent real company names, salaries or job links as facts.\n  Label salary ranges as \"approximate\".\nDefine TypeScript types for every AI response in `types/ai.ts`.\n\nAI features and their JSON schemas:\n\nA) Career Recommendations\n   input: { interests[], skills[], education, preferredWorkStyle }\n   output: { careers: [ { title, matchPercent, whyItFits, requiredSkills[],\n             missingSkills[], approxSalaryRangeINR, growthOutlook } ] }  (3-5 items)\n\nB) Resume Review\n   input: resume data object\n   output: { score (0-100), strengths[], weaknesses[],\n             suggestions: [ { section, issue, improvedText } ],\n             atsKeywordsMissing[] }\n\nC) Mock Interview\n   input: { role, difficulty, type: \"technical\" | \"hr\" | \"mixed\" }\n   output: { questions: [ { id, question, type, idealAnswerPoints[] } ] } (8 items)\n   plus: evaluateAnswer(question, userAnswer) -> { score (0-10), feedback,\n         improvedAnswer }\n\nD) Learning Roadmap\n   input: { targetRole, currentSkills[], weeksAvailable }\n   output: { phases: [ { title, weeks, topics[], projectIdea,\n             freeResources: [ { name, type } ] } ] }\n   (resource names are generic like \"official docs\", never fabricated URLs)\n\n=====================================================\n4. DATA MODELS (keep exactly these)\n=====================================================\nUser { id, name, email, role: \"student\"|\"admin\", createdAt }\nProfile { userId, education, branch, year, interests[], careerGoal, bio }\nResume { userId, personal{name,email,phone,linkedin,github,location},\n         summary, education[], experience[], projects[], skills[],\n         certifications[] }\nSkill { id, userId, name, category: \"technical\"|\"soft\", level: 1-5 }\nCertificate { id, userId, title, issuer, date, fileDataUrl? }\nApplication { id, userId, company, role, type: \"internship\"|\"job\",\n              status: \"saved\"|\"applied\"|\"interview\"|\"offer\"|\"rejected\",\n              appliedDate, notes }\nRoadmapPlan { id, userId, targetRole, data, createdAt }\n\n=====================================================\n5. PHASES (build ONLY phase 1 now, then stop)\n=====================================================\n\nPHASE 1: Foundation + Auth + Profile\n- App shell, routing, sidebar layout, landing page with hero + feature grid.\n- Register / Login / Logout with the auth abstraction. Role-based route guard.\n- Profile page: view/edit form with validation.\n- Seed one demo student and one demo admin (show credentials on login page\n  labelled \"demo\").\nDONE WHEN: I can register, log in, edit my profile, refresh the page and still\nbe logged in, log out, and a student cannot open /admin.\n\nPHASE 2: AI Career Recommendations (schema A)\n- Form pre-filled from Profile + Skills. Button \"Get Recommendations\".\n- Results as cards with match % bar, missing skills chips, and a\n  \"Create roadmap for this\" button (links to Phase 5 later).\n- Save last result.\nDONE WHEN: bad network shows an error + retry; results render from valid JSON;\na malformed response does not crash the page.\n\nPHASE 3: Resume Builder + PDF + Resume Review (schema B)\n- Multi-section form with live preview side by side.\n- \"Download PDF\" via jsPDF producing a clean, single-column, ATS-friendly\n  layout (no images, standard headings).\n- \"AI Review\" shows score ring, strengths, weaknesses, and per-section\n  suggestions with an \"Apply suggestion\" button.\nDONE WHEN: PDF text is selectable, fits A4, and applying a suggestion updates\nthe form and preview.\n\nPHASE 4: Skill Tracker + Certificates\n- Add/edit/delete skills with level slider, grouped by technical/soft.\n- Certificates list with add form and optional file attach\n  (store as data URL for now).\nDONE WHEN: CRUD works and persists across refresh.\n\nPHASE 5: Mock Interview (schema C) + Learning Roadmap (schema D)\n- Interview: choose role/difficulty/type, answer one question at a time,\n  get AI score + feedback, show session summary.\n- Roadmap: generate, display as a vertical timeline, allow checking off\n  topics, save progress.\nDONE WHEN: a full 8-question session completes with a final average score,\nand roadmap checkbox progress persists.\n\nPHASE 6: Internship/Job Tracker + Progress Dashboard\n- Kanban board by status (drag-and-drop or simple move buttons).\n- Dashboard: profile completion %, skills count, resume score, applications\n  by status, roadmap progress, simple charts (use recharts).\nDONE WHEN: moving a card updates dashboard numbers instantly.\n\nPHASE 7: Admin Dashboard\n- Users table (search, deactivate), counts of platform usage,\n  and a simple \"Career Resources\" CRUD (title, category, link, description)\n  that students can see on a Resources page.\nDONE WHEN: admin changes are visible to students.\n\n=====================================================\n6. UX RULES\n=====================================================\n- Every list has an empty state with a call to action.\n- Every async action has a loading state; every failure has a human-readable\n  message.\n- Forms validate before submit. Accessible labels, keyboard focus visible.\n- No lorem ipsum; use realistic demo content.\n\n=====================================================\n7. OUTPUT FORMAT FOR EACH PHASE\n=====================================================\n1) Assumptions and plan (short)\n2) File tree of what you added/changed\n3) The code\n4) \"Done when\" checklist with PASS/FAIL\n5) One sentence: what I should test manually\nThen STOP and wait.\n\nBegin now with PHASE 1 only.\n[AIS_METADATA_SECTION_START]\nInstructions: If you need more context from the user you must first ask the user some questions using the ask_question tool. If you ask questions, you must wait for the user\'s answers. After the questions you must present a plan.\n[AIS_METADATA_SECTION_END]"
    items {
      text: "You are a senior full-stack engineer building \"Career Compass\", an AI-powered\ncareer guidance and resume builder web app for college students (BCA level).\n\n=====================================================\n0. HOW YOU MUST WORK (read first, obey always)\n=====================================================\n1. THINK BEFORE CODING. Before writing code for each phase, state in 3-6 lines:\n   your assumptions, what you will build, and what you will NOT build.\n   If something is ambiguous, pick the simplest reasonable interpretation\n   and say so. Do not silently guess.\n2. SIMPLICITY FIRST. Write the minimum code that solves the problem.\n   No speculative features, no over-abstraction, no unused dependencies.\n   Small readable components (<150 lines each). Plain, boring, working code\n   beats clever code.\n3. SURGICAL CHANGES. When I ask for a change later, touch only the files\n   needed. Do not refactor or restyle unrelated code.\n4. GOAL-DRIVEN. Every phase ends with a checklist of \"done when\" criteria.\n   Verify each item yourself and report PASS/FAIL before moving on.\n5. NO FAKE FEATURES. If a button exists, it must work. If something is\n   mocked, label it clearly with a \"TODO: mocked\" comment and tell me.\n6. BUILD ONLY THE CURRENT PHASE. Stop after each phase and wait for me to say\n   \"next\".\n\n=====================================================\n1. PRODUCT IN ONE PARAGRAPH\n=====================================================\nStudents currently use scattered websites for career choice, resumes,\ninterview prep and skill tracking. Career Compass puts it all in one place:\na student registers, fills a career profile, gets AI career recommendations,\nbuilds and downloads a resume as PDF, tracks skills and certificates,\npractices mock interview questions, follows an AI learning roadmap, and tracks\ninternship/job applications. An admin manages users and content.\n\nTwo roles: STUDENT and ADMIN.\n\n=====================================================\n2. TECH STACK AND CONSTRAINTS\n=====================================================\nFrontend: React + TypeScript (or JS if simpler), Tailwind CSS, React Router.\nPDF: jsPDF.\nAI: Gemini API (use the Gemini SDK). The API key must NEVER be hard-coded or\nexposed in client-visible source. Use the environment/secret mechanism of this\nplatform, and route AI calls through a single module `services/ai.ts`\nso the provider can be swapped later.\nData layer (Phase 1 only): use an abstraction `services/db.ts` with functions\nlike getProfile(), saveProfile(), listSkills() backed by localStorage for now.\nAuth (Phase 1 only): simple email+password mock auth with a `services/auth.ts`\nabstraction. I will replace both with Express + MongoDB + JWT later, so\nkeep the function signatures clean and async.\nDesign: clean, modern, mobile-responsive, indigo/slate palette, card-based\nlayout, sidebar navigation on desktop, bottom/hamburger nav on mobile.\nMust look like a real product, not a template demo.\n\n=====================================================\n3. AI CONTRACT RULES (critical)\n=====================================================\nEvery Gemini call must:\n- Use a system instruction defining the role.\n- Request JSON ONLY, with a fixed schema (use responseMimeType\n  \"application/json\" and a responseSchema where supported).\n- Be wrapped in try/catch with: loading state, error state with Retry button,\n  and a JSON-parse fallback.\n- Never invent real company names, salaries or job links as facts.\n  Label salary ranges as \"approximate\".\nDefine TypeScript types for every AI response in `types/ai.ts`.\n\nAI features and their JSON schemas:\n\nA) Career Recommendations\n   input: { interests[], skills[], education, preferredWorkStyle }\n   output: { careers: [ { title, matchPercent, whyItFits, requiredSkills[],\n             missingSkills[], approxSalaryRangeINR, growthOutlook } ] }  (3-5 items)\n\nB) Resume Review\n   input: resume data object\n   output: { score (0-100), strengths[], weaknesses[],\n             suggestions: [ { section, issue, improvedText } ],\n             atsKeywordsMissing[] }\n\nC) Mock Interview\n   input: { role, difficulty, type: \"technical\" | \"hr\" | \"mixed\" }\n   output: { questions: [ { id, question, type, idealAnswerPoints[] } ] } (8 items)\n   plus: evaluateAnswer(question, userAnswer) -> { score (0-10), feedback,\n         improvedAnswer }\n\nD) Learning Roadmap\n   input: { targetRole, currentSkills[], weeksAvailable }\n   output: { phases: [ { title, weeks, topics[], projectIdea,\n             freeResources: [ { name, type } ] } ] }\n   (resource names are generic like \"official docs\", never fabricated URLs)\n\n=====================================================\n4. DATA MODELS (keep exactly these)\n=====================================================\nUser { id, name, email, role: \"student\"|\"admin\", createdAt }\nProfile { userId, education, branch, year, interests[], careerGoal, bio }\nResume { userId, personal{name,email,phone,linkedin,github,location},\n         summary, education[], experience[], projects[], skills[],\n         certifications[] }\nSkill { id, userId, name, category: \"technical\"|\"soft\", level: 1-5 }\nCertificate { id, userId, title, issuer, date, fileDataUrl? }\nApplication { id, userId, company, role, type: \"internship\"|\"job\",\n              status: \"saved\"|\"applied\"|\"interview\"|\"offer\"|\"rejected\",\n              appliedDate, notes }\nRoadmapPlan { id, userId, targetRole, data, createdAt }\n\n=====================================================\n5. PHASES (build ONLY phase 1 now, then stop)\n=====================================================\n\nPHASE 1: Foundation + Auth + Profile\n- App shell, routing, sidebar layout, landing page with hero + feature grid.\n- Register / Login / Logout with the auth abstraction. Role-based route guard.\n- Profile page: view/edit form with validation.\n- Seed one demo student and one demo admin (show credentials on login page\n  labelled \"demo\").\nDONE WHEN: I can register, log in, edit my profile, refresh the page and still\nbe logged in, log out, and a student cannot open /admin.\n\nPHASE 2: AI Career Recommendations (schema A)\n- Form pre-filled from Profile + Skills. Button \"Get Recommendations\".\n- Results as cards with match % bar, missing skills chips, and a\n  \"Create roadmap for this\" button (links to Phase 5 later).\n- Save last result.\nDONE WHEN: bad network shows an error + retry; results render from valid JSON;\na malformed response does not crash the page.\n\nPHASE 3: Resume Builder + PDF + Resume Review (schema B)\n- Multi-section form with live preview side by side.\n- \"Download PDF\" via jsPDF producing a clean, single-column, ATS-friendly\n  layout (no images, standard headings).\n- \"AI Review\" shows score ring, strengths, weaknesses, and per-section\n  suggestions with an \"Apply suggestion\" button.\nDONE WHEN: PDF text is selectable, fits A4, and applying a suggestion updates\nthe form and preview.\n\nPHASE 4: Skill Tracker + Certificates\n- Add/edit/delete skills with level slider, grouped by technical/soft.\n- Certificates list with add form and optional file attach\n  (store as data URL for now).\nDONE WHEN: CRUD works and persists across refresh.\n\nPHASE 5: Mock Interview (schema C) + Learning Roadmap (schema D)\n- Interview: choose role/difficulty/type, answer one question at a time,\n  get AI score + feedback, show session summary.\n- Roadmap: generate, display as a vertical timeline, allow checking off\n  topics, save progress.\nDONE WHEN: a full 8-question session completes with a final average score,\nand roadmap checkbox progress persists.\n\nPHASE 6: Internship/Job Tracker + Progress Dashboard\n- Kanban board by status (drag-and-drop or simple move buttons).\n- Dashboard: profile completion %, skills count, resume score, applications\n  by status, roadmap progress, simple charts (use recharts).\nDONE WHEN: moving a card updates dashboard numbers instantly.\n\nPHASE 7: Admin Dashboard\n- Users table (search, deactivate), counts of platform usage,\n  and a simple \"Career Resources\" CRUD (title, category, link, description)\n  that students can see on a Resources page.\nDONE WHEN: admin changes are visible to students.\n\n=====================================================\n6. UX RULES\n=====================================================\n- Every list has an empty state with a call to action.\n- Every async action has a loading state; every failure has a human-readable\n  message.\n- Forms validate before submit. Accessible labels, keyboard focus visible.\n- No lorem ipsum; use realistic demo content.\n\n=====================================================\n7. OUTPUT FORMAT FOR EACH PHASE\n=====================================================\n1) Assumptions and plan (short)\n2) File tree of what you added/changed\n3) The code\n4) \"Done when\" checklist with PASS/FAIL\n5) One sentence: what I should test manually\nThen STOP and wait.\n\nBegin now with PHASE 1 only.\n[AIS_METADATA_SECTION_START]\nInstructions: If you need more context from the user you must first ask the user some questions using the ask_question tool. If you ask questions, you must wait for the user\'s answers. After the questions you must present a plan.\n[AIS_METADATA_SECTION_END]"
