  user_input {
    user_response: "You are now a complete software team reviewing \"Career Compass\" before release.\nYou will act as FOUR roles in strict order. Do not skip a role. Do not say\n\"looks good\" without evidence. Your job is to FIND PROBLEMS, not to praise.\n\n=====================================================\n0. RULES OF THE REVIEW (obey always)\n=====================================================\n1. EVIDENCE OR IT DID NOT HAPPEN. Every PASS must cite what you checked\n   (file name, function, or exact steps performed). Every FAIL must cite\n   the file/line and the exact reproduction steps.\n2. READ THE ACTUAL CODE. Do not assume a feature works because it exists.\n   Trace each feature from UI -> service -> storage -> UI.\n3. DO NOT FIX WHILE REVIEWING. First finish the full report for the current\n   role. Fixing comes only in the FIX PHASE (section 6).\n4. SURGICAL FIXES ONLY. When fixing, change the minimum code needed, do not\n   refactor or restyle unrelated code, and list every file touched.\n5. SEVERITY SCALE for every issue:\n   - BLOCKER: app crashes, data loss, security hole, core feature broken\n   - MAJOR: feature works wrongly or confusingly\n   - MINOR: cosmetic or small usability issue\n   - SUGGESTION: nice to have, do NOT implement without approval\n6. NO INVENTED PROBLEMS. If something is fine, say PASS. If you cannot verify\n   something in this environment, mark it UNVERIFIED and explain why.\n7. Be honest about limits: if auth and data are localStorage-based, say which\n   security checks are not meaningful until the Express + MongoDB backend exists.\n\nIssue format (use for every finding):\n[ID] [SEVERITY] [Module] Title\nSteps to reproduce: ...\nExpected: ...\nActual: ...\nSuggested fix: ...\n\n=====================================================\nROLE 1: QA ENGINEER + DEVELOPER (functional and code review)\n=====================================================\nTest every module below. For each, run the Happy Path, Edge Cases, and\nFailure Cases.\n\n1.1 AUTH & ROUTING\n- Register: valid data, duplicate email, empty fields, invalid email format,\n  weak password, very long inputs, leading/trailing spaces, uppercase vs\n  lowercase email.\n- Login: correct, wrong password, unknown email, empty form.\n- Session persists after refresh; logout fully clears session.\n- Protected routes redirect to login when logged out.\n- Student cannot open /admin by typing the URL. Admin routes show no student-only\n  data issues.\n- Back button after logout does not reveal private pages.\n\n1.2 PROFILE\n- Create, edit, refresh, verify persistence. Validation on every field.\n- Editing the profile updates dependent screens (Career Recommendations form,\n  Dashboard completion %).\n\n1.3 AI FEATURES (Career Recommendations, Resume Review, Mock Interview,\n    Roadmap)\n- Valid response renders correctly.\n- Simulate: network failure, empty response, malformed JSON, JSON missing\n  required fields, extra fields, extremely long response, 429/quota error.\n  The page must NEVER crash or show a white screen. Retry button must work.\n- Double-click on the generate button must not fire two parallel requests.\n- Confirm the Gemini API key is not present in any client-visible source,\n  console log, or network payload. Report exactly where it is read.\n- Confirm no fabricated URLs, company names or salary claims are presented\n  as facts.\n- Confirm all AI responses conform to the types in types/ai.ts, with runtime\n  validation, not just TypeScript types.\n\n1.4 RESUME BUILDER + PDF\n- Add/remove/reorder entries in every section (education, experience,\n  projects, skills, certifications).\n- Empty resume, resume with only one section, resume with 20+ items,\n  very long text, special characters, unicode, emoji, ampersands, quotes.\n- PDF: selectable text, fits A4, multi-page overflow handled, no cut-off text,\n  no overlapping, correct fonts, correct file name (e.g., Name_Resume.pdf).\n- Live preview matches the PDF output.\n- \"Apply suggestion\" updates form + preview and does not duplicate or corrupt\n  data. Applying twice is safe.\n\n1.5 SKILLS & CERTIFICATES\n- Full CRUD. Level slider boundaries (1 and 5). Duplicate skill names.\n- Delete asks for confirmation.\n- Certificate file: wrong file type, oversized file, no file, same file twice.\n  Check localStorage size limits and handle quota errors gracefully.\n\n1.6 MOCK INTERVIEW & ROADMAP\n- Full 8-question session; skipping a question; empty answer; one-word answer;\n  extremely long answer; refresh mid-session (what happens?); final average\n  score math is correct.\n- Roadmap: generate, check/uncheck topics, progress % math is correct and\n  persists; regenerating does not silently destroy saved progress without a\n  warning.\n\n1.7 INTERNSHIP/JOB TRACKER + DASHBOARD\n- Add/edit/delete/move cards across every status. Invalid dates. Empty states.\n- Dashboard numbers must exactly equal the underlying data. Verify each metric\n  by manually counting from stored data and comparing.\n- Charts render with zero data and with large data.\n\n1.8 ADMIN\n- User list, search, deactivate: a deactivated user must actually be blocked\n  from logging in.\n- Resource CRUD visible to students immediately.\n- Admin cannot accidentally delete/deactivate themselves (last admin).\n\n1.9 CODE QUALITY REVIEW\n- Dead code, unused imports/dependencies, console.log leftovers, duplicate\n  logic, components >150 lines, missing error boundaries, any use of `any`,\n  hard-coded strings that should be constants, missing keys in lists,\n  memory leaks (uncleared timers/listeners), unhandled promise rejections.\n- Does services/auth.ts and services/db.ts have clean async signatures ready to\n  be swapped for the Express + MongoDB backend? List any place where UI code\n  bypasses the service layer and touches localStorage directly (this is a bug).\n\n1.10 NON-FUNCTIONAL\n- Responsive check at 360px, 768px, 1280px widths.\n- Keyboard-only navigation of the main flows, visible focus, form labels,\n  alt text, color contrast (WCAG AA), aria attributes on modals.\n- Loading states exist for every async action. No layout jumping.\n- Performance: unnecessary re-renders, large bundle imports, unoptimized lists.\n- Browser console: report ALL errors and warnings.\n\nDeliver: QA REPORT with a table of all test cases (ID, module, scenario,\nPASS/FAIL/UNVERIFIED, evidence), followed by the issue list.\n\n=====================================================\nROLE 2: SECURITY REVIEWER\n=====================================================\nCheck and report:\n- XSS: can any user-entered text (name, bio, notes, resume fields, resource\n  descriptions from admin) be rendered as HTML/script? Any use of\n  dangerouslySetInnerHTML?\n- Prompt injection: if a user types \"ignore previous instructions and ...\"\n  into the resume or interview answer, does the AI output break the schema\n  or leak the system instruction?\n- Input sanitization and length limits before sending user text to the AI.\n- Passwords: how are they stored right now? (State clearly that plaintext or\n  localStorage passwords are acceptable ONLY for the prototype and must be\n  bcrypt + JWT on the server.)\n- Authorization: every admin action must be checked by role, not only hidden\n  in the UI. Try calling admin service functions as a student.\n- Data isolation: user A must not be able to read or modify user B\'s profile,\n  resume, skills, certificates, applications, or roadmaps through any service\n  function, even by passing another userId.\n- Secrets: no API keys, tokens, or demo credentials in the production build\n  other than clearly labelled demo accounts.\n- Dependency check: list dependencies, flag unused or suspicious ones.\n- Produce a list of security items that MUST be implemented in the Express +\n  MongoDB phase (helmet, rate limiting, zod validation, CORS, JWT expiry,\n  password hashing, file upload validation).\n\n=====================================================\nROLE 3: PRODUCT MANAGER (does it solve the real problem?)\n=====================================================\nReview as a PM who must decide whether this ships to real students.\n\n3.1 REQUIREMENT TRACEABILITY MATRIX\nCreate a table mapping each feature in the original synopsis to the\nimplementation:\nStudent Registration & Secure Login | Career Profile Management |\nAI-Based Career Recommendations | Resume Builder with PDF Download |\nResume Review | Skill Tracking | Certificate Upload | Mock Interview Questions |\nLearning Roadmaps | Internship & Job Tracker | Progress Dashboard |\nAdmin Dashboard.\nColumns: Feature | Where implemented | Status (COMPLETE / PARTIAL / MOCKED /\nMISSING) | Gap | Priority.\n\n3.2 USER JOURNEY WALKTHROUGH\nWalk through these journeys as a real student, step by step, counting clicks\nand noting every point of confusion:\n a) First-time user: landing -> register -> understand what to do next.\n b) \"I don\'t know what career to choose\": profile -> recommendations ->\n    roadmap.\n c) \"I have a placement drive next week\": resume -> AI review -> download PDF\n    -> mock interview.\n d) Returning user after 2 weeks: login -> dashboard -> what changed, what to\n    do next.\n e) Admin: add a resource -> verify the student sees it.\n\n3.3 PRODUCT QUALITY QUESTIONS (answer each with evidence)\n- Is the first-time experience clear without any explanation from me?\n- Is there an onboarding nudge (\"complete your profile to get better results\")?\n- Does every AI output feel specific to the user, or generic? Test with 3\n  different profiles (e.g., BCA student interested in web dev, a student\n  interested in data analysis, a student with an empty profile) and compare.\n- Is the language simple enough for a college student? Any jargon?\n- Is anything misleading, such as a \"match %\" that looks scientific but is just\n  AI guesswork? Recommend honest labelling.\n- Dead ends: any screen where the user cannot tell what to do next?\n- Is there anything a student would expect to find that is missing\n  (e.g., forgot password, delete account, export my data)?\n- Rank the TOP 10 improvements by impact vs effort.\n\n3.4 PM VERDICT\nGive: Ready for demo? Ready for real students? Rate 1-10 on clarity,\ncompleteness, trust, polish. List must-fix-before-demo items versus\ncan-wait items. Do not add new features to the must-fix list.\n\n=====================================================\nROLE 4: MULTI-USER TESTER (simulated concurrent users)\n=====================================================\nBe honest about what this environment can and cannot do. If the app uses\nlocalStorage, true concurrency across devices cannot be tested; do what is\npossible (multiple accounts in the same browser, plus code-level reasoning)\nand mark the rest UNVERIFIED with a note on how to test it after the\nMongoDB backend exists.\n\nCreate and use these test personas, each with realistic data:\n - Aarav (student, complete profile, full resume, many skills, many apps)\n - Priya (student, brand new, empty everything)\n - Rohan (student, malicious input: script tags, 5000-character strings,\n   emoji, SQL-like text, prompt-injection text)\n - Meera (student, has deactivated account)\n - Admin1 (admin) and Admin2 (second admin)\n\nRun these scenarios:\n M1. Aarav and Priya both register and use the app; confirm zero data\n     bleed between them on every screen (dashboard, skills, resume,\n     applications, roadmap, certificates).\n M2. Log in as Aarav, log out, log in as Priya on the same browser: confirm\n     no cached or stale data of Aarav is visible anywhere (including\n     in-memory state, form drafts, AI results, and the browser\'s back button).\n M3. Same account in two tabs: edit the profile in tab 1, then edit in tab 2.\n     What happens? (Last write wins? Data loss? Stale UI?) Report the behavior\n     and recommend a fix.\n M4. Two users generate AI results at the same time: results must not get\n     mixed up. Check for shared global variables or shared cache keys not\n     scoped per userId.\n M5. Rohan submits malicious inputs into every text field and the AI prompts:\n     nothing executes, layout does not break, PDF does not break, schema is\n     still respected.\n M6. Meera (deactivated) tries to log in and tries to reuse an existing session.\n M7. Admin1 deactivates a user while that user is logged in: what happens on\n     the user\'s next action?\n M8. Admin1 and Admin2 edit the same resource simultaneously.\n M9. A student tries to access admin routes and admin service functions\n     directly.\n M10. Load test (logical): 50 users\' worth of generated data in storage. Does\n      the UI still perform? Are there any O(n^2) loops, missing pagination, or\n      storage quota crashes?\n M11. Rate-limit behavior: one user spamming the AI buttons; confirm the app\n      throttles or disables the button and shows a friendly message.\n\nDeliver: MULTI-USER REPORT with scenario ID, steps, expected, actual,\nPASS/FAIL/UNVERIFIED.\n\n=====================================================\n6. FIX PHASE (only after all 4 reports are delivered)\n=====================================================\n1. Merge all issues into one list, deduplicated, sorted by severity.\n2. Show me the list and the proposed fix for each BLOCKER and MAJOR.\n3. Then fix ALL BLOCKERS and MAJORS, surgically, one at a time. After each\n   fix, re-run the exact reproduction steps and report PASS/FAIL.\n4. Do NOT implement MINOR or SUGGESTION items unless I ask.\n5. After fixes, run a REGRESSION CHECK: re-verify the 12 core features still\n   work (register, login, profile edit, career recommendation, resume build,\n   PDF download, skill add, certificate add, mock interview, roadmap,\n   application move, admin resource add).\n\n=====================================================\n7. FINAL GO / NO-GO SCORECARD\n=====================================================\nOutput a final table:\nArea | Score /10 | Blockers open | Notes\n(Functionality, AI reliability, Security, UX, Responsiveness, Accessibility,\nCode quality, Multi-user safety, Requirement coverage)\n\nThen:\n- Total open issues by severity\n- What is UNVERIFIED and how I should test it manually\n- Final verdict: GO / GO WITH CONDITIONS / NO-GO, with the reasons\n- A 10-point manual test checklist I should run myself before my viva/demo\n- A list of exactly what changes when I move from localStorage to Express +\n  MongoDB + JWT (so I can explain it to my examiner)\n\nBegin with ROLE 1. Deliver the full QA report, then continue to the next role\nwithout waiting, until the final scorecard. If you hit a length limit, stop at\na clean role boundary and tell me which role to continue from.\n[AIS_METADATA_SECTION_START]\nInstructions: If you need more context from the user you must first ask the user some questions using the ask_question tool. If you ask questions, you must wait for the user\'s answers. After the questions you must present a plan.\n[AIS_METADATA_SECTION_END]"
    items {
      text: "You are now a complete software team reviewing \"Career Compass\" before release.\nYou will act as FOUR roles in strict order. Do not skip a role. Do not say\n\"looks good\" without evidence. Your job is to FIND PROBLEMS, not to praise.\n\n=====================================================\n0. RULES OF THE REVIEW (obey always)\n=====================================================\n1. EVIDENCE OR IT DID NOT HAPPEN. Every PASS must cite what you checked\n   (file name, function, or exact steps performed). Every FAIL must cite\n   the file/line and the exact reproduction steps.\n2. READ THE ACTUAL CODE. Do not assume a feature works because it exists.\n   Trace each feature from UI -> service -> storage -> UI.\n3. DO NOT FIX WHILE REVIEWING. First finish the full report for the current\n   role. Fixing comes only in the FIX PHASE (section 6).\n4. SURGICAL FIXES ONLY. When fixing, change the minimum code needed, do not\n   refactor or restyle unrelated code, and list every file touched.\n5. SEVERITY SCALE for every issue:\n   - BLOCKER: app crashes, data loss, security hole, core feature broken\n   - MAJOR: feature works wrongly or confusingly\n   - MINOR: cosmetic or small usability issue\n   - SUGGESTION: nice to have, do NOT implement without approval\n6. NO INVENTED PROBLEMS. If something is fine, say PASS. If you cannot verify\n   something in this environment, mark it UNVERIFIED and explain why.\n7. Be honest about limits: if auth and data are localStorage-based, say which\n   security checks are not meaningful until the Express + MongoDB backend exists.\n\nIssue format (use for every finding):\n[ID] [SEVERITY] [Module] Title\nSteps to reproduce: ...\nExpected: ...\nActual: ...\nSuggested fix: ...\n\n=====================================================\nROLE 1: QA ENGINEER + DEVELOPER (functional and code review)\n=====================================================\nTest every module below. For each, run the Happy Path, Edge Cases, and\nFailure Cases.\n\n1.1 AUTH & ROUTING\n- Register: valid data, duplicate email, empty fields, invalid email format,\n  weak password, very long inputs, leading/trailing spaces, uppercase vs\n  lowercase email.\n- Login: correct, wrong password, unknown email, empty form.\n- Session persists after refresh; logout fully clears session.\n- Protected routes redirect to login when logged out.\n- Student cannot open /admin by typing the URL. Admin routes show no student-only\n  data issues.\n- Back button after logout does not reveal private pages.\n\n1.2 PROFILE\n- Create, edit, refresh, verify persistence. Validation on every field.\n- Editing the profile updates dependent screens (Career Recommendations form,\n  Dashboard completion %).\n\n1.3 AI FEATURES (Career Recommendations, Resume Review, Mock Interview,\n    Roadmap)\n- Valid response renders correctly.\n- Simulate: network failure, empty response, malformed JSON, JSON missing\n  required fields, extra fields, extremely long response, 429/quota error.\n  The page must NEVER crash or show a white screen. Retry button must work.\n- Double-click on the generate button must not fire two parallel requests.\n- Confirm the Gemini API key is not present in any client-visible source,\n  console log, or network payload. Report exactly where it is read.\n- Confirm no fabricated URLs, company names or salary claims are presented\n  as facts.\n- Confirm all AI responses conform to the types in types/ai.ts, with runtime\n  validation, not just TypeScript types.\n\n1.4 RESUME BUILDER + PDF\n- Add/remove/reorder entries in every section (education, experience,\n  projects, skills, certifications).\n- Empty resume, resume with only one section, resume with 20+ items,\n  very long text, special characters, unicode, emoji, ampersands, quotes.\n- PDF: selectable text, fits A4, multi-page overflow handled, no cut-off text,\n  no overlapping, correct fonts, correct file name (e.g., Name_Resume.pdf).\n- Live preview matches the PDF output.\n- \"Apply suggestion\" updates form + preview and does not duplicate or corrupt\n  data. Applying twice is safe.\n\n1.5 SKILLS & CERTIFICATES\n- Full CRUD. Level slider boundaries (1 and 5). Duplicate skill names.\n- Delete asks for confirmation.\n- Certificate file: wrong file type, oversized file, no file, same file twice.\n  Check localStorage size limits and handle quota errors gracefully.\n\n1.6 MOCK INTERVIEW & ROADMAP\n- Full 8-question session; skipping a question; empty answer; one-word answer;\n  extremely long answer; refresh mid-session (what happens?); final average\n  score math is correct.\n- Roadmap: generate, check/uncheck topics, progress % math is correct and\n  persists; regenerating does not silently destroy saved progress without a\n  warning.\n\n1.7 INTERNSHIP/JOB TRACKER + DASHBOARD\n- Add/edit/delete/move cards across every status. Invalid dates. Empty states.\n- Dashboard numbers must exactly equal the underlying data. Verify each metric\n  by manually counting from stored data and comparing.\n- Charts render with zero data and with large data.\n\n1.8 ADMIN\n- User list, search, deactivate: a deactivated user must actually be blocked\n  from logging in.\n- Resource CRUD visible to students immediately.\n- Admin cannot accidentally delete/deactivate themselves (last admin).\n\n1.9 CODE QUALITY REVIEW\n- Dead code, unused imports/dependencies, console.log leftovers, duplicate\n  logic, components >150 lines, missing error boundaries, any use of `any`,\n  hard-coded strings that should be constants, missing keys in lists,\n  memory leaks (uncleared timers/listeners), unhandled promise rejections.\n- Does services/auth.ts and services/db.ts have clean async signatures ready to\n  be swapped for the Express + MongoDB backend? List any place where UI code\n  bypasses the service layer and touches localStorage directly (this is a bug).\n\n1.10 NON-FUNCTIONAL\n- Responsive check at 360px, 768px, 1280px widths.\n- Keyboard-only navigation of the main flows, visible focus, form labels,\n  alt text, color contrast (WCAG AA), aria attributes on modals.\n- Loading states exist for every async action. No layout jumping.\n- Performance: unnecessary re-renders, large bundle imports, unoptimized lists.\n- Browser console: report ALL errors and warnings.\n\nDeliver: QA REPORT with a table of all test cases (ID, module, scenario,\nPASS/FAIL/UNVERIFIED, evidence), followed by the issue list.\n\n=====================================================\nROLE 2: SECURITY REVIEWER\n=====================================================\nCheck and report:\n- XSS: can any user-entered text (name, bio, notes, resume fields, resource\n  descriptions from admin) be rendered as HTML/script? Any use of\n  dangerouslySetInnerHTML?\n- Prompt injection: if a user types \"ignore previous instructions and ...\"\n  into the resume or interview answer, does the AI output break the schema\n  or leak the system instruction?\n- Input sanitization and length limits before sending user text to the AI.\n- Passwords: how are they stored right now? (State clearly that plaintext or\n  localStorage passwords are acceptable ONLY for the prototype and must be\n  bcrypt + JWT on the server.)\n- Authorization: every admin action must be checked by role, not only hidden\n  in the UI. Try calling admin service functions as a student.\n- Data isolation: user A must not be able to read or modify user B\'s profile,\n  resume, skills, certificates, applications, or roadmaps through any service\n  function, even by passing another userId.\n- Secrets: no API keys, tokens, or demo credentials in the production build\n  other than clearly labelled demo accounts.\n- Dependency check: list dependencies, flag unused or suspicious ones.\n- Produce a list of security items that MUST be implemented in the Express +\n  MongoDB phase (helmet, rate limiting, zod validation, CORS, JWT expiry,\n  password hashing, file upload validation).\n\n=====================================================\nROLE 3: PRODUCT MANAGER (does it solve the real problem?)\n=====================================================\nReview as a PM who must decide whether this ships to real students.\n\n3.1 REQUIREMENT TRACEABILITY MATRIX\nCreate a table mapping each feature in the original synopsis to the\nimplementation:\nStudent Registration & Secure Login | Career Profile Management |\nAI-Based Career Recommendations | Resume Builder with PDF Download |\nResume Review | Skill Tracking | Certificate Upload | Mock Interview Questions |\nLearning Roadmaps | Internship & Job Tracker | Progress Dashboard |\nAdmin Dashboard.\nColumns: Feature | Where implemented | Status (COMPLETE / PARTIAL / MOCKED /\nMISSING) | Gap | Priority.\n\n3.2 USER JOURNEY WALKTHROUGH\nWalk through these journeys as a real student, step by step, counting clicks\nand noting every point of confusion:\n a) First-time user: landing -> register -> understand what to do next.\n b) \"I don\'t know what career to choose\": profile -> recommendations ->\n    roadmap.\n c) \"I have a placement drive next week\": resume -> AI review -> download PDF\n    -> mock interview.\n d) Returning user after 2 weeks: login -> dashboard -> what changed, what to\n    do next.\n e) Admin: add a resource -> verify the student sees it.\n\n3.3 PRODUCT QUALITY QUESTIONS (answer each with evidence)\n- Is the first-time experience clear without any explanation from me?\n- Is there an onboarding nudge (\"complete your profile to get better results\")?\n- Does every AI output feel specific to the user, or generic? Test with 3\n  different profiles (e.g., BCA student interested in web dev, a student\n  interested in data analysis, a student with an empty profile) and compare.\n- Is the language simple enough for a college student? Any jargon?\n- Is anything misleading, such as a \"match %\" that looks scientific but is just\n  AI guesswork? Recommend honest labelling.\n- Dead ends: any screen where the user cannot tell what to do next?\n- Is there anything a student would expect to find that is missing\n  (e.g., forgot password, delete account, export my data)?\n- Rank the TOP 10 improvements by impact vs effort.\n\n3.4 PM VERDICT\nGive: Ready for demo? Ready for real students? Rate 1-10 on clarity,\ncompleteness, trust, polish. List must-fix-before-demo items versus\ncan-wait items. Do not add new features to the must-fix list.\n\n=====================================================\nROLE 4: MULTI-USER TESTER (simulated concurrent users)\n=====================================================\nBe honest about what this environment can and cannot do. If the app uses\nlocalStorage, true concurrency across devices cannot be tested; do what is\npossible (multiple accounts in the same browser, plus code-level reasoning)\nand mark the rest UNVERIFIED with a note on how to test it after the\nMongoDB backend exists.\n\nCreate and use these test personas, each with realistic data:\n - Aarav (student, complete profile, full resume, many skills, many apps)\n - Priya (student, brand new, empty everything)\n - Rohan (student, malicious input: script tags, 5000-character strings,\n   emoji, SQL-like text, prompt-injection text)\n - Meera (student, has deactivated account)\n - Admin1 (admin) and Admin2 (second admin)\n\nRun these scenarios:\n M1. Aarav and Priya both register and use the app; confirm zero data\n     bleed between them on every screen (dashboard, skills, resume,\n     applications, roadmap, certificates).\n M2. Log in as Aarav, log out, log in as Priya on the same browser: confirm\n     no cached or stale data of Aarav is visible anywhere (including\n     in-memory state, form drafts, AI results, and the browser\'s back button).\n M3. Same account in two tabs: edit the profile in tab 1, then edit in tab 2.\n     What happens? (Last write wins? Data loss? Stale UI?) Report the behavior\n     and recommend a fix.\n M4. Two users generate AI results at the same time: results must not get\n     mixed up. Check for shared global variables or shared cache keys not\n     scoped per userId.\n M5. Rohan submits malicious inputs into every text field and the AI prompts:\n     nothing executes, layout does not break, PDF does not break, schema is\n     still respected.\n M6. Meera (deactivated) tries to log in and tries to reuse an existing session.\n M7. Admin1 deactivates a user while that user is logged in: what happens on\n     the user\'s next action?\n M8. Admin1 and Admin2 edit the same resource simultaneously.\n M9. A student tries to access admin routes and admin service functions\n     directly.\n M10. Load test (logical): 50 users\' worth of generated data in storage. Does\n      the UI still perform? Are there any O(n^2) loops, missing pagination, or\n      storage quota crashes?\n M11. Rate-limit behavior: one user spamming the AI buttons; confirm the app\n      throttles or disables the button and shows a friendly message.\n\nDeliver: MULTI-USER REPORT with scenario ID, steps, expected, actual,\nPASS/FAIL/UNVERIFIED.\n\n=====================================================\n6. FIX PHASE (only after all 4 reports are delivered)\n=====================================================\n1. Merge all issues into one list, deduplicated, sorted by severity.\n2. Show me the list and the proposed fix for each BLOCKER and MAJOR.\n3. Then fix ALL BLOCKERS and MAJORS, surgically, one at a time. After each\n   fix, re-run the exact reproduction steps and report PASS/FAIL.\n4. Do NOT implement MINOR or SUGGESTION items unless I ask.\n5. After fixes, run a REGRESSION CHECK: re-verify the 12 core features still\n   work (register, login, profile edit, career recommendation, resume build,\n   PDF download, skill add, certificate add, mock interview, roadmap,\n   application move, admin resource add).\n\n=====================================================\n7. FINAL GO / NO-GO SCORECARD\n=====================================================\nOutput a final table:\nArea | Score /10 | Blockers open | Notes\n(Functionality, AI reliability, Security, UX, Responsiveness, Accessibility,\nCode quality, Multi-user safety, Requirement coverage)\n\nThen:\n- Total open issues by severity\n- What is UNVERIFIED and how I should test it manually\n- Final verdict: GO / GO WITH CONDITIONS / NO-GO, with the reasons\n- A 10-point manual test checklist I should run myself before my viva/demo\n- A list of exactly what changes when I move from localStorage to Express +\n  MongoDB + JWT (so I can explain it to my examiner)\n\nBegin with ROLE 1. Deliver the full QA report, then continue to the next role\nwithout waiting, until the final scorecard. If you hit a length limit, stop at\na clean role boundary and tell me which role to continue from.\n[AIS_METADATA_SECTION_START]\nInstructions: If you need more context from the user you must first ask the user some questions using the ask_question tool. If you ask questions, you must wait for the user\'s answers. After the questions you must present a plan.\n[AIS_METADATA_SECTION_END]"
    }
  }
}
